 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Eviction uses 2Q. First access places an entry in queue A1. A second access moves the entry to queue A2 and then to queue B as a reused item. Eviction removes from A1 first, then B, protecting entries that have been accessed repeatedly from one-off sequential screening scans. Queue size split between A1/A2 and B is a tunable parameter. All replacement bookkeeping stays private to the worker and does not change the external preview API, render queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Protects comparison sheets reused across screening sessions from eviction during long sequential scans. Accepts extra bookkeeping for two queues and a size split. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Queue size split values for A1/A2 vs B and tuning procedure for different productions.
+2. Atomicity guarantees when moving entries between queues under concurrent access.
+3. Metrics to observe hit rate for reused vs one-use entries and signal for split retuning.
